$ C:\Users\Lawre\covenant\.venv\Scripts\python.exe test_v1_verifier_agreement.py
   V1 -- three witnesses, actually compared
   
         available  constitution.py    464b1975bc05988e26bf4044
         available  verify.ps1         464b1975bc05988e26bf4044
         available  verify.sh          464b1975bc05988e26bf4044
   
   ok    N1 AT LEAST TWO verifiers ran. One implementation agreeing with itself is not agreement, and an absent verifier must never be counted as a concurring one -- the same rule this project applies to a silent witness everywhere else
   ok    N2 ...and any verifier that could NOT run is named, so a reader knows which implementations this result actually covers
   ok    A1 every verifier that ran produced the SAME root, character for character. They share no code and no language, so agreement here is evidence rather than a tautology
   ok    A2 the published anchor is readable and holds a root
   ok    A3:constitution.py    agrees with the PUBLISHED anchor, not merely with the other verifiers. Three implementations could agree with each other and all be wrong about what was published
   ok    A3:verify.ps1         agrees with the PUBLISHED anchor, not merely with the other verifiers. Three implementations could agree with each other and all be wrong about what was published
   ok    A3:verify.sh          agrees with the PUBLISHED anchor, not merely with the other verifiers. Three implementations could agree with each other and all be wrong about what was published
   ok    D1 verify.sh invokes neither python nor constitution.py in its CODE. Agreement between two implementations where one calls the other is one implementation agreeing with itself
   ok    D2 verify.ps1 invokes neither python nor verify.sh in its code. It NAMES them in its header, because explaining what it replaces requires naming it -- which is why this reads code and not prose
   ok    D3 each verifier computes sha256 with its OWN toolchain: hashlib, a shell sha256 utility, and .NET's SHA256 respectively
   ok    D4:verify.sh  CALLS no command that Windows System32 also provides. On Windows the tool a shell finds depends on which process launched it, so a verifier that calls one is a verifier whose answer depends on its caller
   ok    D4:check.sh   CALLS no command that Windows System32 also provides. On Windows the tool a shell finds depends on which process launched it, so a verifier that calls one is a verifier whose answer depends on its caller
   ok    C1 both one-command checkers are SHIPPED. The README and both outreach letters tell a stranger to run one of these, so a missing file is a broken instruction on a platform this host cannot test
   ok    C2 AT LEAST ONE checker ran here and reported a verdict. This is the part that holds on every platform: the front door the README sends people to must work on the host running this suite
   ok    C2b the two checkers reach the SAME verdict on this tree -- same passed/disagreed/skipped, same exit code. A Windows reader and a POSIX reader must not be told different things about the same repository
   ok    C3 on an unmutated copy the checker exits 0. Without this, C4 could pass because the checker fails on everything
   ok    C4 altering ONE protected line makes the checker exit non-zero and print MISMATCH. This is the property every invitation to verify depends on, and it is now tested on every run instead of once by hand
   
